  planner_response {
    response: "Both errors come from a Chrome extension installed in your browser (`chrome-extension://eppiocemhmnlbhjplcgkofciiegomcon`, in `executors/200.js` and `libs/requests.js`), not from the Petalnode application.\n\n### Cause\n- The extension\'s request interceptor tries to overwrite `window.fetch` (`window.fetch = ...`) at page load.\n- Inside the preview iframe, `window.fetch` is defined by the host environment with only a getter (read-only accessor), so the extension\'s assignment throws `TypeError: Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter`.\n- A search across the codebase (`index.html`, `vite.config.ts`, `src/`, and `scripts/`) confirms the app itself never assigns to or modifies `window.fetch` or `defineProperty`.\n\n### How to Clear It\n- Disable or pause that Chrome extension on this preview URL (or test in an Incognito window with extensions disabled); since extensions inject at `document_start` before application scripts run and `window.fetch` is managed by the preview host, no app code change is needed or safe to override it."
    thinking: "**Clarifying Code Integrity**\n\nMy initial assessment is complete: the Petalnode codebase is remarkably clean. There are zero instances of suspicious methods like `fetch` or `defineProperty` within the app code, and the main application renders directly without errors. Now, the priority is on clearly and concisely explaining the root cause of the previous two errors, along with the user-side resolution.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "gnwAa-efBfWhz7IPqfTTyQs"
    modified_response: "Both errors come from a Chrome extension installed in your browser (`chrome-extension://eppiocemhmnlbhjplcgkofciiegomcon`, in `executors/200.js` and `libs/requests.js`), not from the Petalnode application.\n\n### Cause\n- The extension\'s request interceptor tries to overwrite `window.fetch` (`window.fetch = ...`) at page load.\n- Inside the preview iframe, `window.fetch` is defined by the host environment with only a getter (read-only accessor), so the extension\'s assignment throws `TypeError: Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter`.\n- A search across the codebase (`index.html`, `vite.config.ts`, `src/`, and `scripts/`) confirms the app itself never assigns to or modifies `window.fetch` or `defineProperty`.\n\n### How to Clear It\n- Disable or pause that Chrome extension on this preview URL (or test in an Incognito window with extensions disabled); since extensions inject at `document_start` before application scripts run and `window.fetch` is managed by the preview host, no app code change is needed or safe to override it."
