  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Initiating Senior Review**\n\nI\'m diving into a senior-level review of the procedural material implementations across `textures.ts`, `IslandBlocks.tsx`, `StorySetPieces.tsx`, and `DevTextures.tsx`. I\'m focused on evaluating the current state and identifying opportunities to enhance the voxel block materials for greater richness, crispness, and realism.\n\n\n**Analyzing Quality Tiers**\n\nI\'m zeroing in on `paintBlockBuffers`, `getBlockMaterial`, and `IslandBlocks`. I\'ve realized the `QualityTier` parameter is ignored. This is a chance to make performance-aware decisions. For lower tiers, I plan to skip normal and roughness maps, keeping fast albedo and baked shading. Higher tiers will enable the full PBR relief for desktop. My testing confirms that a `64x64` resolution fits the pixel-voxel aesthetic.\n\n\n**Refining Texture Details**\n\nI\'m now focusing on `DevTextures.tsx` and the visual inspection from `dev-textures-v2.png`. I\'m identifying specific improvements needed to the materials. For example, for `glow_ore`, I\'ll adjust the emissive intensity, incorporate faceted bevel shading and subtle slate rim highlights to enhance the glowing crystal veins. With `cherry_leaves`, I\'ll improve the edge shadows to avoid gray mixed pixels using alpha mips.\n\n\n**Improving Blossom Textures**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the cherry blossom textures to make them more visually appealing. The goal is to make soft, layered rosettes with clean translucent voids, rather than noisy clusters. I\'m focusing on richer color gradients and avoiding muddy grays in the shadows. For the cherry log, I\'m adjusting the bark\'s color palette to keep the rich woodgrain and lenticel bands legible under ACES Filmic + N8AO shadows. Lastly, I\'m also addressing the repetitive dots in the dirt and grass textures to introduce more organic soil patterns.\n\n\n**Enhancing Voxel Material**\n\nI\'m now focusing on texture improvements. The dirt texture is improved with natural clods, faint root fibers, warm umber pebbles, and moisture patches. Stone and deep stone textures now use larger, clean masonry blocks with mineral bands, chisel marks, and quartz flecks. The cherry planks get subtle hand-inked joinery chamfers and warm wood-ring knots. Water will have multi-layered animated caustics and subtle shoreline foam.\n\n\n**Verifying UV Normal Space**\n\nMy current focus is ensuring the Y sign of the Sobel normal map is correct when dealing with `tex.flipY = true` within `textures.ts`. I need to confirm the consistent application of tangent-space coordinate, particularly regarding how the green channel (`ny`) in Three.js corresponds to the `+v` direction. I\'ve been tracing the Sobel formula to ensure upward-facing bevels are correctly represented. I\'m double-checking the relationship between image buffer coordinates and UV coordinates to avoid flipped normals. I am using a test case with a bumped surface.\n\n\n**Confirming Normal Direction**\n\nI\'m now certain I\'ve corrected the Y sign in the Sobel normal map within `textures.ts`. My key insight was that, given that the height increases as you go *down* the Y-axis, a positive SobelY value corresponds to an upward tilt in the normal vector. Previously, I was inverting the Y-axis unnecessarily. Now, every single surface detail aligns correctly with the lighting.\n\n\n**Implementing Color Variance**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented color variation across the island\'s blocks. By using `InstancedMesh.instanceColor`, I\'m achieving \302\2615\342\200\2237% hue/luminance variations on textures, which avoids tiling repetition. It is achieved with zero extra draw calls or memory usage. I\'m focusing on ensuring this preserves the warmth of the `seedGrassMat` and generates cherry blossom blocks with gentle color variations. I\'m also carefully tuning `material.envMapIntensity` for each material type to preserve contrast and vibrancy.\n\n\n**Reviewing Asset Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating texture upgrades into existing assets. I need to enhance Sprout\'s trunk and leaves with subtle roughness, or maintain their clean sketchbook aesthetic for compatibility. I\'m upgrading `/dev/textures` to display all 14 blocks cleanly, plus material diagnostics and tier toggles. I\'ll verify high-detail PBR and low-tier fallback performance in the inspector.\n\n\n"
