  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the User\'s Request**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the user\'s instructions. I\'m focusing on the specifics of `/dev/textures` and the core goal: updating the page to showcase the voxel materials. The emphasis is on side-by-side comparisons of the original and the new normal-mapped versions of the textures. I\'ll need to account for both sets of visuals.\n\n\n**Integrating Height Maps**\n\nI\'m now focused on seamlessly integrating height map generation within the material pipeline, specifically for \'stone\' and \'bark\' textures. I\'m leveraging the existing `height: Float32Array` from `PaintedBlockBuffers`, to explore how to generate an explicit `heightMap: Uint8Array` as a grayscale displacement/bump texture. The goal is cleaner, more efficient, and visually compelling depth and lighting response, without external asset dependencies. I\'m considering using RGBA data for a compact grayscale heightmap.\n\n\n**Refining Height Map Generation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on integrating a configurable `grainIntensity` parameter to modulate micro-grain imperfections in albedo, height, and roughness within the material pipeline. I\'ll need to scale the `normalScale` / `bumpScale` strength accordingly. My current plan is to wire the generated height map into `getBlockMaterial` for stone and bark materials. I need to clarify how Three.js handles the interaction of `normalMap` and `bumpMap` within `MeshStandardMaterial`.\n\n\n**Investigating Texture Integration**\n\nI\'m now investigating how to genuinely integrate the height map into the material pipeline alongside the normal map. Specifically, to enhance depth and lighting response for \'stone\' and \'cherry_log\' (bark). Three.js\'s WebGL shader reveals a crucial detail: the `bumpMap` is ignored when a `normalMap` is set. To address this, I\'m considering height-derived cavity ambient occlusion and multi-octave height-field relief options for a robust solution.\n\n\n**Exploring Heightmap Integration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on leveraging `aoMap` for height-derived ambient occlusion, and exploring the potential of a deterministic `onBeforeCompile` hook. I think I can use this approach to add height-field relief effects with almost no GPU cost, focusing on `\'stone\'`, `\'deep_stone\'`, and `\'cherry_log\'`. I\'m going to look into how I can read `uHeightMap` and see if the effect is seamless. I\'m also considering integrating directly into `textures.ts`, if I can.\n\n\n**Implementing Height-Based AO**\n\nI\'m now implementing height-based cavity self-occlusion and tangent view-ray micro-parallax within the GLSL shader. I\'m prioritizing clean compilation and zero WebGL warnings in Three.js `0.187.0`. I\'m also exploring a simpler approach using 3D geometry-clean `uv1` and the native Three.js `aoMap` combined with height-driven Sobel normal synthesis, which should avoid shader maintenance issues and work natively with various lighting and rendering features. I\'ve confirmed that Three.js r151+ defaults `aoMap` to `uv1`.\n\n\n"
