    thinking: "**Synthesizing Project Audit**\n\nI\'ve achieved a complete repository overview, encompassing every file, test, and TODO. With that full project view, I\'m now synthesizing the **Phase 1 Audit** data. I\'m focusing on systematically planning subsequent phases.\n\n\n**Analyzing Release Readiness**\n\nI\'ve completed a thorough examination of every item flagged by `npm run check:release`. That includes scrutinizing the `57 TODOs` (specifically `18 Blocking URL TODOs` and `39 Fact / Copy TODOs`). I have also examined every page and route. I can report I\'ve already confirmed the solid implementation of the core offerings, including the Minecraft tiers, the rewards system, and key brand URLs. This ensures a solid foundation for release.\n\n\n**Evaluating Bot Hosting Plans**\n\nI\'m now diving into the unfinished bot hosting work. Specifically, I\'m concentrating on the `/bots` route. The goal is to design a well-structured bot hosting plan lineup. I\'m focusing on crafting a progression of cohesive tiers with associated RAM, CPU, storage, and pricing information, bearing in mind practical use-cases and pricing in INR.\n\n\n**Defining Bot Hosting Specs**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the specifications for the bot hosting plans. I\'m focusing on the technical details: determining required RAM, CPU, storage, and pricing in INR, considering a logical upgrade progression. I will also construct a comparison table and feature breakdown for clarity. The infrastructure is strictly Miami, USA VPS based, and I need to update `Locations.tsx` and `src/content/pages.ts` to reflect this and mark the bot hosting infrastructure as \"Draft\". All specs, pricing, runtimes, and checkout links are to be clearly marked as \"DRAFT / AWAITING OWNER APPROVAL,\" disabling checkout buttons if necessary and directing users to inquire on Discord.\n\n\n**Addressing Critical TODOs**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the `StickerButton.tsx`, `PlanCard.tsx`, and `VpsHosting.tsx` files and found a critical bug where placeholder \"TODO\" URLs in production render misleading purchase buttons, even with `e.preventDefault()`. These buttons currently lack visual indicators, `aria-disabled`, or right-click safeguards to prevent navigating to a broken link. I\'m now crafting a robust solution that will ensure checkout is disabled and directs users to Discord or billing inquiry, while marking all as \"DRAFT / AWAITING OWNER APPROVAL\".\n\n\n**Refining Button Handling**\n\nI\'m now revising the approach to handle draft bot hosting plans. I realize I need to refine the checkout button logic. The goal is to prevent broken links, and clearly mark plans as draft, instead of relying on `e.preventDefault()`. I\'ll replace the existing checkout buttons on draft plans with a disabled \"Draft \342\200\224 Pending Approval\" button, accompanied by a Discord link. This ensures the tests in `sections.test.tsx` on `PlanCard` remain valid. I must remember to extend that logic to the `VpsHosting` component.\n\n\n**Integrating Bot Hosting Plans**\n\nI\'m now integrating bot hosting plans. I\'ve focused on ensuring the `PlanCard` renders the correct link state. I will ensure that the integration with the `VpsHosting` component functions as expected. I\'m focusing on preserving compatibility with the existing tests in `sections.test.tsx` and adding appropriate `aria-disabled` attributes and informative messages to the buttons when the plans are drafts. I will also incorporate clear guidance to use the Open Billing Store or Discord for pending checkout links, and will update the homepage to reflect the integration.\n\n\n**Updating Homepage Content**\n\nI\'m now focused on the homepage. I\'ll add a new \"Hosting Categories\" section right below `PlansHighlight`, showcasing Minecraft, KVM VPS, and Bot Hosting, linking to `/plans`, `/vps`, and `/bots`, while retaining the existing Minecraft cards. I\'m also reviewing `brand.metaTitle`, `brand.oneLiner`, and `story` for integration, and will ensure all existing tests pass, particularly regarding the `story` data within the homepage content.\n\n\n**Verifying Latency Claims**\n\nI\'m now investigating latency claims and Miami location details within the codebase. I\'m running `grep` searches across the entire repository to uncover any existing latency specifications, datacenter names, IP addresses, or network probe results. If these don\'t exist, I will refrain from making any assumptions and note that no verified ping measurement or datacenter probe source exists in the repository. I\'ll stick to a truthful description of the infrastructure. I will also flag verification of the `~2ms` latency claim as an explicit owner verification item before any published figures. I will finalize VPS hosting after this is complete.\n\n\n**Expanding VPS Offering**\n\nI\'m now expanding the `/vps` page. The existing cards, comparison table, and infrastructure box are present, but a comparison of Minecraft vs. VPS vs. Bot Hosting is now necessary. I will implement a use-case breakdown by tier and workload, and address location availability across Singapore, Miami, and India. I will add VPS-specific FAQs and handle unconfirmed direct checkout links as per prior conventions.\n\n\n**Refining VPS Links**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the `/vps` page. My objective is to replace the broken \"Deploy VPS\" CTAs. I\'ll replace the existing button with a clear note, or route to `links.store` for purchases, with the original tracked TODO left in-place. My next step will be to extend these changes to the buttons on the `/bots` route.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Documentation Content**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on documentation. I\'ve broken down Phase 6 into twelve focused topics. I\'ll prioritize these 12 areas, including getting started guides with a practical panel walkthrough and region selection, creating Minecraft servers with engine options, Java and Bedrock connection guidance, installing plugins, file management, and server backups. I am creating honest `TODO` notes for owner confirmation, prioritizing practical guidance and safe testing.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Documentation Guides**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on documentation. I have prioritized getting started guides, including a practical panel walkthrough and region selection, creating Minecraft servers with engine options, Java and Bedrock connection guidance, installing plugins, file management, and server backups. I am creating honest `TODO` notes for owner confirmation, focusing on practical guidance and safe testing. I\'m focusing on VPS Getting Started and Bot Hosting Getting Started guides.\n\n\n**Enhancing Site Navigation**\n\nI\'m now focused on site navigation, and improving the structure of `/terms`, `/privacy`, and `/sla` pages. I intend to add direct cross-navigation tabs/links in the page header and create a table of contents to enable quick anchor jumps for easier navigation. I will craft richer, well-structured, honest draft clauses. Finally, I will clearly mark all unconfirmed legal details for owner review.\n\n\n**Evaluating Site Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now diving into site enhancements, particularly responsiveness around 1024px-1280px and the mobile drawer. I\'ll thoroughly review `TrustStrip.tsx`, `FAQ.tsx`, and `RewardsSection.tsx` to eliminate any bugs or missing data. Furthermore, I will meticulously examine `BotHosting.tsx` to address the hardcoded \"TODO\" entries and incorporate crucial information, including Miami location details, use-case guidance, and comparisons against VPS/Minecraft. I\'ll then scrutinize `App.tsx` and the `ROUTE_META` to enrich SEO elements and per-route `JSON-LD` schemas as recommended. Finally, I will compare `npm run check:release` against `listTodos()` in `src/content/index.ts`.\n\n\n"
