  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Confirming Project Status**\n\nI\'m incredibly pleased to see the codebase\'s cleanliness, making the missing elements strikingly obvious. A thorough sweep with `grep` confirmed the absence of any Miami-specific latency measurements, IPs, traceroutes, or datacenter facility references. That validates the Phase.\n\n\n**Designing Implementation Phases**\n\nI\'m now meticulously architecting the implementation across several phases, specifically focusing on a 5-tier botanical progression for the bot hosting. I\'m prioritizing a centralized content structure in `src/content/index.ts` and `src/content/pages.ts`, ensuring all elements seamlessly integrate with the existing codebase and tests, while adhering to the Cherry Blossom aesthetic. Furthermore, I\'m ensuring there are no universal claims of `2ms` ping, opting instead for transparent and honest language, along with explicitly flagging the need for verification of network measurements within our internal documentation.\n\n\n**Defining Pricing Tiers**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the botanical tier names, ensuring no overlap with existing systems. I\'m carefully comparing the suggested pricing against Minecraft and VPS costs to ensure a fair and competitive market entry, with a focus on shared resources and burstable performance on the Miami VPS. I\'ve drafted the *Pollen* tier pricing at \342\202\27139/mo for the first bot, and I\'m looking at other options.\n\n\n**Finalizing Pricing Tiers**\n\nI\'ve just structured the pricing tiers and associated resources for the remaining plans. The *Dewdrop* tier offers 1GB RAM, shared CPU, and 6GB NVMe at \342\202\27169/mo. The *Nectar* tier steps up with 2GB RAM, 150% shared vCPU, and 10GB NVMe for \342\202\271119/mo. Finally, *Petal* provides 4GB RAM, 200% shared vCPU, and 15GB NVMe at \342\202\271199/mo. Each includes an intended use-case.\n\n\n**Defining Final Tier**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the top tier, *Leaf*, offering 6GB RAM, 250% shared vCPU (with fair-use limitations), and 25GB NVMe for \342\202\271279/mo. This positions it strategically just below the \342\202\271300/mo VPS, which clearly indicates the value progression. Furthermore, I\'ve confirmed that a transition to a dedicated Root KVM VPS offers clear advantages. I\'ve also added safeguards to mark the proposed tier as \"Draft\" and clarified guarantees and limitations. I will mention the Miami infrastructure.\n\n\n**Specifying Infrastructure Location**\n\nI\'m now detailing the infrastructural nuances. I\'ve explicitly set `botHosting.infrastructureLocation` to `\"Miami, USA (VPS Infrastructure)\"` and added `miamiLatencyTodo` for a crucial measurement check. I\'m taking care not to publish any unverified latency figures. Furthermore, I\'ve split `botHosting.capabilities` into `confirmed` (managed via panel.petalnodes.cloud with existing standard features) and `proposed` (runtime containers and auto-features) segments; the latter is explicitly labeled \"Proposed for launch \342\200\224 pending final egg/runtime verification.\" The `paymenterUrl` still points to TODO tasks.\n\n\n**Refining Homepage Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the three core product categories into the homepage. Specifically, I\'m exporting `hostingCategories` from `src/content/index.ts`, to describe Minecraft, VPS, and Bot Hosting. The Minecraft tier starts at \342\202\27159/mo and is available in Singapore, Miami, and India. I\'ve also just modified `sections.test.tsx`, updating the necessary assertions.\n\n\n\n**Designing Comparison Tables**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a reusable `<HostingComparisonBand />` component to clearly differentiate Minecraft, VPS, and Bot Hosting. This will highlight key aspects like the best use-case, level of control, runtimes, locations, pricing tiers, and direct product links. I\'ll integrate this band directly into the homepage (`Home.tsx`) and the VPS and Bot Hosting pages to provide concise information and upgrade paths.\n\n\n**Expanding VPS Product Page**\n\nI\'m now enriching the `/vps` page. I\'m focusing on five well-defined plans with specific resources and pricing, along with targeted use-case recommendations for each tier. I\'m also adding location and hardware notices, and implementing honest checkout handling with a TODO state for payment links.\n\n\n**Implementing Payment Redirects**\n\nI\'ve just added a fallback for VPS plan purchase links. If a direct plan link is a TODO, the \"Order VPS\" button now routes to the main billing store, with a clear secondary note indicating the direct link is pending. Tests confirm this works, avoiding any dead links on the /vps page, while still tracking the individual checkout URL TODOs.\n\n\n**Adding `/plans` Enhancements**\n\nI\'m expanding the `/plans` implementation. I\'ve realized `PlanCard` already preserves its `href`, which avoids test breaks. I\'m enhancing it with `aria-disabled` and a clear \"Checkout URL pending\" tooltip when the payment URL is a TODO, or a fallback link. Furthermore, I will create a VPS FAQs section right on `/vps` using an accordion, covering key aspects, and add a comparison section for Minecraft, VPS, and Bot Hosting.\n\n\n**Defining Documentation Topics**\n\nI\'m now detailing documentation topics, specifically enriching the `/docs` page. I\'m dynamically updating the `Docs.tsx` chapter list to reflect content categories, while ensuring the search functionality encompasses article notes and tags. I\'m focusing on **12 priority chapters** in `src/content/pages.ts`, starting with a detailed \"Getting Started\" chapter, and the rest covering server creation, connection options, engine specifics, plugin installations, file management, and server backups.\n\n\n**Expanding Documentation Content**\n\nI\'m now detailing documentation topics, specifically enriching the `/docs` page, focusing on new VPS and Bot Hosting \"Getting Started\" chapters. I\'m dynamically updating the `Docs.tsx` chapter list to reflect content categories, ensuring the search encompasses article notes and tags. I\'m prioritizing the updated chapters, including verified KVM basics and Miami-based container panel workflow, maintaining clarity on supported runtimes, with precise labels and clear TODO notes on pre-launch content.\n\n\n**Structuring Legal Updates**\n\nI\'m now focusing on legal and policy pages, specifically enhancing `LegalPage.tsx` with a quick policy switcher and an \"On This Page\" table of contents. I\'m adding section IDs and visual distinctions between confirmed service practices and those requiring owner approval. I\'m also enriching `legalDocs` in `src/content/pages.ts` with thorough, realistic draft sections, never inventing unconfirmed legal details.\n\n\n**Updating Page Integrations**\n\nI\'m now integrating new content. I\'ve updated Miami\'s location page with a \"Bot Hosting (Draft Infrastructure)\" mention in `src/content/pages.ts`, explicitly noting this is a Miami-exclusive service, excluding unverified ping claims. I\'m also refining SEO metadata and descriptions across routes in `src/App.tsx` and `index.html`. Furthermore, I\'ll add dynamic JSON-LD structured data within `RouteMetaAndScrollSync` to optimize SEO across key pages.\n\n\n"
