    thinking: "**Confirming \"Pollen (Draft)\"**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed a critical detail: keeping \"Pollen (Draft)\" visible is guaranteed by a specific test assertion. The test at `sections.test.tsx:187` explicitly checks for its inclusion in the bot HTML, so I think this keeps the name \"Pollen (Draft)\" visible cleanly on the card/heading/test. This means the proposed name is validated.\n\n\n**Refining Content Configuration**\n\nI\'m now carefully editing `/src/content/index.ts` and `/src/content/pages.ts`, meticulously reviewing details for clarity and accuracy. I\'ve double-checked the content, including `brand.oneLiner`, `brand.metaTitle`, and `brand.metaDescription` ensuring they\'re crisp and concise, especially the metaTitle. I\'m also ensuring the hosting categories and bot hosting information are correctly configured, and all key attributes are represented.\n\n\n**Detailing Infrastructure Notes**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the infrastructure specifics. I\'ve documented the location as Miami, USA, noting VPS infrastructure. Key infrastructure elements like NVMe storage and network DDoS protection are clarified in the \'infrastructureNote\'. I\'ve also added a TODO about verifying the Miami latency claim. I\'m noting the draft status with a draftNotice, and have added proposed naming details, as well as notes regarding supported runtimes.\n\n\n**Defining Bot Hosting Plans**\n\nI\'m presently outlining the bot hosting plans, and have started with `pollen` and `dewdrop` tiers. I\'m focusing on defining capabilities like application start/stop/restart, live console & logs, and file management, all essential for bot management. I\'ve also detailed the fair use policy for vCPU and RAM. The `capabilitiesProposed` are next on my list, specifically verifying 24/7 uptime, runtime presets, and dependency installation. I also need to verify snapshot backups are scheduled, as well.\n\n\n**Outlining Bot Hosting Tiers**\n\nI am now detailing the bot hosting tiers, including their RAM, vCPU allocation, NVMe storage, and pricing. I\'m noting the descriptions for each, such as \"Active moderation, ticket & webhook bots on one server.\" Each tier is in draft status, with the approval requirement. I\'ve also added the todo note about owner approval for pricing.\n\n\n**Expanding Use Cases & FAQs**\n\nI\'m now expanding the documentation. I\'m adding `useCases` for both Bot Hosting and VPS sections, outlining concrete workloads such as Discord moderation and Minecraft proxy servers. I\'m also adding crucial FAQs for both sections, covering topics like runtime status, KVM isolation, and launch timelines. The KVM VPS use cases and region notes are added to the section, as well.\n\n\n**Comparing Hosting Services**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a `serviceComparison` section to showcase Minecraft, VPS, and Bot Hosting, carefully curating data for a shared `<HostingComparison />` component. I\'m detailing \"Best For\", \"Resource Model\", \"Control & Access\", \"Regions Available\", \"Pricing Range\", and the associated action links. I\'m simultaneously updating `/src/content/index.ts` and `/src/content/pages.ts`, and running a check on the former for clean TypeScript and forbidden words.\n\n\n"
